#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167704} :activity/id #uuid "9f886d85-2df1-5f0a-8d7d-9ac4d46bc4e4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048610 :topic/id #uuid "aea1ea1e-f383-548f-9c13-1a066dc606ed" :topic/math-academy-id 3256} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166607 :question/id #uuid "3d0b8fbe-a118-5ec7-83d4-863d605bed74" :question/math-academy-id "e-8375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24e9c062-13d1-5a36-ad70-f062f3765100" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049311}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526103}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsch"} :step/id #uuid "39ddcb2e-7a38-5abc-b491-af74837bc920" :step/math-academy-id 31215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166608 :question/id #uuid "b72eee89-1abb-5081-b89e-6cc8735b7f54" :question/math-academy-id "e-8376"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6071303-6be5-5636-a675-66d33864a7c9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526105}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arsech"} :step/id #uuid "acbc6382-12bf-5cfc-ad37-6f0b89b9fa84" :step/math-academy-id 31216 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166609 :question/id #uuid "35322f22-4526-53dd-9839-83dd4c286776" :question/math-academy-id "e-8377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c7d3082-d63a-5eac-9a9b-47681f8d4694" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526058}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcoth"} :step/id #uuid "b08bfe8b-9e44-5137-a3d9-56d3138b6315" :step/math-academy-id 31217 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168094 :tutorial/content "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}, &  & \\,x\\in (0,1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we arrive at the following basic integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (0,1) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use these formulas for finding integrals. For example, let's find the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3x\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + \\cos x \\right) \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of integration and the formulas above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{3x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77d4740c-3cac-5695-aa67-597e3c0ce164" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5213 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "36d709c0-168f-57e9-9ba0-9651411edeb8" :step/math-academy-id 32698 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168102 :tutorial/content "Recall that \n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = {\\color{blue}\\artanh{x}} + C, \\qquad {\\color{blue}x\\in(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nBut we have just seen that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = {\\color{red}\\arcoth{x}} + C, \\qquad {\\color{red}x\\in(-\\infty,-1) \\cup (1,\\infty)}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the integral on the left-hand side is the same but we used different functions on the right. What is the difference, and how do we know which hyperbolic function to use?\n\nThe difference is the domain of the variable $x.$ When the $|x| < 1,$ we use the antiderivative $\\artanh{x},$ otherwise we use the antiderivative $\\arcoth{x}.$\n\nFor instance, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the limits of integration lie in the interval $(-1,1),$ we used the antiderivative $\\artanh{x}.$\n\nWhile, on the other hand, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the limits of integration lie in the interval $(1,\\infty),$ we used the antiderivative $\\arcoth{x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169c2d19-f0a1-557e-88ed-519503440186" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5227 :tutorial/title "The Importance of the Domain When Integrating Using Inverse Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "6157bd21-aca9-574c-a8ba-03e7aad7c93f" :step/math-academy-id 32720}] :activity/title "Integration Using Inverse Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167022}]}
